<section xml:id="sec-ch04-03-DoubleIntegralsRectangularRegions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Double Integrals in Cartesian Coordinates</title>
  <subtitle>Rectangular Regions</subtitle>

  <note>
    <p>
      In the previous section, we learned how to integrate a surface
      <m>f(x, y)</m> over a curve <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle x(t), y(t)\rangle</m>
      in its domain (to compute net area). In this section, we will learn
      how to integrate <m>f(x, y)</m> over a rectangular region <m>R</m> in
      its domain.
    </p>
  </note>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Geometrically, integrating a function <m>f(x, y)</m> over a
      rectangular region <m>R</m> in its domain computes the net volume
      bounded by <m>f</m> and the <m>x y</m>-plane, restricted to <m>R</m>.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can find the net volume bounded by <m>f</m> and the
      <m>x y</m>-plane, restricted to a rectangle <m>R</m>, by partitioning
      the net volume into rectangular prisms.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-03-prisms">
    <caption>The net volume bounded by f on R, approximated using 4, then 16, then 100 rectangular prisms.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_18_34c12496fbb38c89a591g-1(1). -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-03-prisms" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_10_18_34c12496fbb38c89a591g-1-1.png">
          <shortdescription>The net volume bounded by f on R, approximated using 4, then 16, then 100 rectangular prisms.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>The net volume bounded by <m>f</m> on <m>R</m>, approximated using 4, then 16, then 100 rectangular prisms.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <note>
    <p>
      As the image above suggests, to approximate the net volume bounded by
      <m>f</m> and the <m>x y</m>-plane on <m>R</m> (restricted to <m>R</m>
      ), we simply add up the net volumes of each individual rectangular
      prism.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    Geometrically, we are partitioning the rectangle <m>R</m> in the
    <m>x y</m>-plane into smaller rectangles as pictured below:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-03-partitioned-rectangle">
    <caption>The rectangular region R in the xy-plane partitioned into smaller rectangles, and a sample point.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_18_34c12496fbb38c89a591g-1. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-03-partitioned-rectangle" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_10_18_34c12496fbb38c89a591g-1.jpg">
          <shortdescription>The rectangular region R in the xy-plane partitioned into smaller rectangles, and a sample point.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>The rectangular region <m>R</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane partitioned into smaller rectangles, and a sample point.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <p>
    From the picture, we can see that the area of the <m>k</m>-th rectangle
    is <m>\Delta A_{k}= \Delta x_{k} \Delta y_{k}</m>. Since the height is
    given by the function evaluated at ( <m>x_{k}, y_{k}</m> ), the net
    volume of the <m>k</m>-th rectangular prism is
    <m>f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right) \Delta A_{k}</m>. It follows that an
    approximation for the net volume of the whole region is
    <md>
      V \approx \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right) \Delta A_{k}
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    If we take the number <m>n</m> of rectangles to approach infinity (and
    hence <m>\Delta A_{k} \rightarrow</m> 0 ), we get that the net volume
    is
    <md>
      V=\lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right) \Delta A
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    We call this formula for the net volume a double integral.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Double Integral</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        A function <m>f(x, y)</m> defined on a rectangular region <m>R</m>
        is said to be integrable on <m>R</m> if
        <md>
          \lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right) \Delta A_{k}
        </md>
        exists. Moreover, if this limit exists, we call it the double
        integral of <m>f</m> over <m>R</m>, and write it as
        <md>
          \lim _{n \rightarrow \infty} \sum_{k=1}^{n} f\left(x_{k}, y_{k}\right) \Delta A_{k}=\iint_{R} f(x, y) d A
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        <m>\iint_{R} f(x, y) d A</m> gives the net volume bounded by
        <m>f(x, y)</m> and the <m>x y</m>-plane, on <m>R</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Suppose we want to find the net volume bounded by <m>f</m> and the
      <m>x y</m> plane, on the region
      <m>R=\{(x, y) \mid a \leq x \leq b, c \leq y \leq d\}</m> (we always
      assume <m>a \neq b</m>, <m>c \neq d</m> ).
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Partition the net volume into vertical cross-sections parallel
            to the <m>y</m>-axis.
          </p>

          <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-03-cross-sections-y">
            <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, section 5.1</caption>

            <image source="/images/2025_10_18_34c12496fbb38c89a591g-3.jpg" width="50%">
              <shortdescription>A slice of the net volume bounded by f on R partitioned into vertical cross-sections parallel to the y-axis.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>A slice of the net volume bounded by <m>f</m> on <m>R</m> partitioned into vertical cross-sections parallel to the <m>y</m>-axis.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </figure>

          <p>
            Note that to get each cross section, we simply intersect the
            surface <m>f</m> with a plane <m>x=c_{0}</m> (treat <m>x</m> as
            a constant), <m>a \leq c_{0} \leq b</m>. Hence, we denote the
            net area of each cross section as <m>A(x)</m> (we get a
            different net area/cross section for each <m>x</m> ).
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            To find <m>A(x)</m> (the net area of each cross section), we
            simply integrate <m>f</m> with respect to <m>y</m> (treat
            <m>x</m> as a constant) from <m>c</m> to <m>d</m>. That is,
            <md>
              A(x)=\int_{c}^{d} f(x, y) d y
            </md>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Finally, we want to add up <m>A(x)</m>, for <m>a \leq x \leq b</m>.
            We do this by integrating <m>A(x)</m> with respect to <m>x</m>.
            Hence, the net volume is given by
            <md>
              <mrow>V \amp =\int_{a}^{b} A(x) d x</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp =\int_{a}^{b}\left(\int_{c}^{d} f(x, y) d y\right) d x</mrow>
              <mrow>\amp =\int_{a}^{b} \int_{c}^{d} f(x, y) d y d x</mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <p>
    We summarize this result below.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Evaluating Double Integrals using Iterated Integrals</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        To integrate a function <m>f(x, y)</m> over the rectangular region
        <m>R=\{(x, y) \mid a \leq x \leq b, c \leq y \leq d\}</m>, we use the
        formula
        <md>
          \iint_{R} f(x, y) d A=\int_{a}^{b} \int_{c}^{d} f(x, y) d y d x
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        We call stacked integrals like these iterated integrals.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <note>
    <p>
      Remember to evaluate iterated integrals inside out, that is,
      <md>
        \int_{a}^{b} \int_{c}^{d} f(x, y) d y d x=\int_{a}^{b}\left(\int_{c}^{d} f(x, y) d y\right) d x
      </md>
    </p>
  </note>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-03-ex-16-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate <m>\iint_{R}(6-3 x^2-2y) d A</m> where
        <m>R=\{(x, y) \mid 0 \leq x \leq 1,0 \leq y \leq 2\}</m>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-03-ex-16-1">
        <caption>The surface f of x and y equals 6 minus 3 x squared minus 2 y, above the rectangular region R consisting of all points x, y with x between 0 and 1 and y between 0 and 2.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_18_34c12496fbb38c89a591g-5. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-03-ex-16-1" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_18_34c12496fbb38c89a591g-5.png">
              <shortdescription>The surface f of x and y equals 6 minus 3 x squared minus 2 y, above the rectangular region R consisting of all points x, y with x between 0 and 1 and y between 0 and 2.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The surface <m>f(x, y)=6-3x^2-2y</m>, above the rectangular region <m>R</m> consisting of all points <m>(x, y)</m> with <m>x</m> between 0 and 1 and <m>y</m> between 0 and 2.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        <m>f(x, y)=6-3 x^2-2y</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The region <m>R</m> is the rectangle <m>0\leq x\leq 1</m>,
        <m>0\leq y\leq 2</m>, so we evaluate the double integral as an
        iterated integral in the order <m>dy\,dx</m>:
        <md>
          \iint_{R}(6-3x^2-2y)\, dA = \int_0^1 \int_0^2 (6-3x^2-2y)\, dy\, dx
        </md>.
        Evaluating the inner integral with <m>x</m> treated as a constant:
        <md>
          \int_0^2 (6-3x^2-2y)\, dy = \left. (6-3x^2)y - y^2 \right|_0^2 = (6-3x^2)(2) - 4 = 8 - 6x^2
        </md>.
        Then integrating with respect to <m>x</m>:
        <md>
          \int_0^1 (8-6x^2)\, dx = \left. 8x - 2x^3 \right|_0^1 = 8 - 2 = 6
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      In our method for evaluating double integrals using iterated
      integrals, we could have partitioned the net volume into cross
      sections parallel to the <m>x</m>-axis (treating <m>y</m> as a
      constant):
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-03-cross-sections-x">
      <caption>From OpenStax, Calculus Volume 3, section 5.1</caption>

      <image source="/images/2025_10_18_34c12496fbb38c89a591g-6.jpg" width="50%">
        <shortdescription>A slice of the net volume bounded by f on R partitioned into vertical cross-sections parallel to the x-axis.</shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>A slice of the net volume bounded by <m>f</m> on <m>R</m> partitioned into vertical cross-sections parallel to the <m>x</m>-axis.</p>
        </description>
      </image>
    </figure>
  </note>

  <p>
    With this set up, the net area of the cross sections <m>A(y)</m>, is
    given by
    <md>
      \int_{a}^{b} f(x, y) d x
    </md>
    and the net volume is given by
    <md>
      <mrow>V \amp =\int_{c}^{d} A(y) d y</mrow>
      <mrow>\amp =\int_{c}^{d}\left(\int_{a}^{b} f(x, y) d x\right) d y</mrow>
    </md>
  </p>

  <p>
    When integrating over rectangular regions, either set up will give you
    the same result!
  </p>

  <theorem>
    <title>Fubini's Theorem (Rectangular Regions)</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>f(x, y)</m> be continuous on the rectangular region
        <m>R=\{(x, y) \mid a \leq x \leq b, c \leq y \leq d\}</m>. The double
        integral of <m>f</m> over <m>R</m> can be evaluated by either of two
        iterated integrals:
        <md>
          \iint_{R} f(x, y) d A=\int_{a}^{b} \int_{c}^{d} f(x, y) d y d x=\int_{c}^{d} \int_{a}^{b} f(x, y) d x d y
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-03-ex-16-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate <m>\iint_{R}(6-3 x^2-2y) d A</m> where
        <m>R=\{(x, y) \mid 0 \leq x \leq 1,0 \leq y \leq 2\}</m>
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        This is the same integral as in
        <xref ref="example-ch04-03-ex-16-1"/>; by Fubini's theorem we may
        integrate in the opposite order <m>dx\,dy</m> and obtain the same
        value:
        <md>
          \iint_{R}(6-3x^2-2y)\, dA = \int_0^2 \int_0^1 (6-3x^2-2y)\, dx\, dy
        </md>.
        Evaluating the inner integral with <m>y</m> treated as a constant:
        <md>
          \int_0^1 (6-3x^2-2y)\, dx = \left. 6x - x^3 - 2yx \right|_0^1 = 6 - 1 - 2y = 5 - 2y
        </md>.
        Then integrating with respect to <m>y</m>:
        <md>
          \int_0^2 (5-2y)\, dy = \left. 5y - y^2 \right|_0^2 = 10 - 4 = 6
        </md>,
        which matches the value found in
        <xref ref="example-ch04-03-ex-16-1"/>, exactly as Fubini's theorem
        guarantees.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Changing the order of integration in an iterated integral can
      significantly reduce the difficulty of the problem.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-03-ex-16-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate <m>\int_{-1}^{1} \int_{0}^{1} x^3 \sin(e^y) d y d x</m>
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-03-ex-16-3">
        <caption>The surface f of x and y equals x cubed times the sine of e to the power y, and the net volume bounded by f and the xy-plane.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_18_34c12496fbb38c89a591g-8. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-03-ex-16-3" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_18_34c12496fbb38c89a591g-8.png">
              <shortdescription>The surface f of x and y equals x cubed times the sine of e to the power y, and the net volume bounded by f and the xy-plane.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The surface <m>f(x, y)=x^3\sin(e^y)</m>, and the net volume bounded by <m>f</m> and the <m>xy</m>-plane.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The surface <m>f(x, y)=x^3 \sin(e^y)</m> in blue and the net volume
        in orange
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        Integrating in the given order would require
        <m>\int_0^1 \sin(e^y)\, dy</m>, which has no elementary
        antiderivative. Since the region is the rectangle
        <m>-1\leq x\leq 1</m>, <m>0\leq y\leq 1</m>, Fubini's theorem lets
        us reverse the order of integration:
        <md>
          \int_{-1}^{1} \int_{0}^{1} x^3 \sin(e^y)\, dy\, dx = \int_{0}^{1} \int_{-1}^{1} x^3 \sin(e^y)\, dx\, dy
        </md>.
        Now <m>\sin(e^y)</m> is constant with respect to <m>x</m>, so the
        inner integral is
        <md>
          \int_{-1}^{1} x^3 \sin(e^y)\, dx = \sin(e^y) \left. \frac{x^4}{4} \right|_{-1}^{1} = \sin(e^y)\left(\frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{4}\right) = 0
        </md>.
        The inner integral vanishes for every <m>y</m> because <m>x^3</m>
        is odd and the interval <m>[-1,1]</m> is symmetric about the
        origin. Hence
        <md>
          \int_{0}^{1} 0\, dy = 0
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
